    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:DefinedTerm",
      "@id": "https://japan-cannabinoid-trends.routeflags.com/taxonomy/compound_thcb",
      "schema:name": "THCB",
      "schema:inDefinedTermSet": {
        "@type": "schema:DefinedTermSet",
        "@id": "https://japan-cannabinoid-trends.routeflags.com/taxonomy/compound-thcb-scheme"
      },
      "schema:description": "'THCB' is the owner's term, registered as a search string on 2026-11-11 by owner direction (ISS-20261111-TAX-040). Its chemical identity is UNCONFIRMED: the repository records no verified chemical name, structure, chemical class, natural occurrence, route/origin, synonym relation or regulatory status for this term, and no external identifier (CAS, ChEBI, Wikidata, PubChem) was verified - none is recorded here and none may be inferred from the query string. The term sits in compound_identity_unverified, an administrative registration-state category, so this placement asserts no route or chemical classification. What the code 'THCB' expands to is not recorded anywhere in this repository, so no chemical name is supplied. On 2026-11-11 the owner confirmed that the generic chemical interpretation of `THCB` is replaced by three distinct search-topic concepts: `Δ8-THCB`, `Δ9-THCB` and `THCB-O-acetate` (ISS-20261111-TAX-041). This concept remains the registration of the literal string `THCB`: the `thcb-general-ja` / `thcb-liquid-ja` query entries and every adopted or preserved run stay attached to this concept and are not reassigned to any new concept. The generic term is neither a synonym of nor a parent of the three concepts, and no `skos:related` or `skos:exactMatch` link is asserted; the new concepts are separate registrations. Evidence that would move the term out of this category: a documented chemical name with a verified external identifier, or a recorded source naming the substance and its route/origin; the basis belongs in docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md.",
      "schema:termCode": "compound_thcb"
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:DefinedTerm",
      "@id": "https://japan-cannabinoid-trends.routeflags.com/taxonomy/compound_thcp",
      "schema:name": "THCP",
      "schema:inDefinedTermSet": {
        "@type": "schema:DefinedTermSet",
        "@id": "https://japan-cannabinoid-trends.routeflags.com/taxonomy/compound-thcp-scheme"
      },
      "schema:description": "`THCP` is the owner's term, registered as a search string on 2026-11-11 by owner direction (ISS-20261111-TAX-040). On 2026-11-11 the owner confirmed the intended meaning of the registered term: the tetrahydrocannabiphorol C7 homologue family, with the Δ8/Δ9 positional isomer UNSPECIFIED (ISS-20261111-TAX-040 addendum). This fixes the search-topic family only. Δ8-THCP and Δ9-THCP are separate substances, the registered string identifies neither isomer, and no Google Trends or YouTube result is attributed to an isomer. No single external record denotes the family, so this concept carries no skos:exactMatch and no CAS number, PubChem CID, UNII, ChEBI ID or Wikidata QID: the per-isomer records are documented in docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md as evidence about the isomers, never as this concept's identifier. The single-substance chemical identity remains UNCONFIRMED and the term stays in compound_identity_unverified, an administrative registration-state category, so this placement asserts no route/origin, natural occurrence, chemical class, synonym or regulatory status; the family scope recorded here is the term's documented scope, not a chemical-class link. The published chemistry is recorded in the ledger with its limits: Citti et al. 2019 (DOI 10.1038/s41598-019-56785-1) reports Δ9-THCP as a C7 homologue, and the frequently repeated 33-fold figure is a CB1 binding-affinity ratio, not human potency. ISS-20261015-TAX-027 recorded a decision not to register this term because no name, route placement or identifier evidence existed; the owner's 2026-11-11 scope decision supersedes that decision for this term (ISS-20261111-TAX-040). The COA panel entry records THCP as not detected in one sample, which is an observation about that sample, not a placement basis. **Query limitation:** Google Trends and YouTube series for `THCP` / `THCP リキッド` measure occurrences of those literal strings; `THCP` is an acronym that can match unrelated text, and a matching result is not evidence of tetrahydrocannabiphorol content. Evidence that would move the term out of this category: a documented single chemical name with a verified external identifier, or a recorded source naming the single substance and its route/origin; the basis belongs in docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md.",
      "schema:termCode": "compound_thcp"
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:DefinedTerm",
      "@id": "https://japan-cannabinoid-trends.routeflags.com/taxonomy/compound_thcjd",
      "schema:name": "THCJD",
      "schema:inDefinedTermSet": {
        "@type": "schema:DefinedTermSet",
        "@id": "https://japan-cannabinoid-trends.routeflags.com/taxonomy/compound-thcjd-scheme"
      },
      "schema:description": "`THCJD` is the owner's term, registered as a search string on 2026-11-11 by owner direction (ISS-20261111-TAX-040). On 2026-11-11 the owner confirmed the intended meaning of the registered term: the THC-JD / 3-octyl-THC C8 homologue family, with the Δ8/Δ9 positional isomer UNSPECIFIED (ISS-20261111-TAX-040 addendum). This fixes the search-topic family only. The spelling `THCJD` (no hyphen) is the owner's search string, not an authoritative registry name; the naming and the Δ8/Δ9 3-octyl-THC identification are recorded in Tanaka & Kikura-Hanajiri 2024 (DOI 10.1248/yakushi.24-00029). The Δ8 and Δ9 3-octyl-THC forms are separate substances, the registered string identifies neither isomer, and no Google Trends or YouTube result is attributed to an isomer. No single external record denotes the family, so this concept carries no skos:exactMatch and no CAS number, PubChem CID, UNII, ChEBI ID or Wikidata QID: the per-isomer records are documented in docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md as evidence about the isomers, never as this concept's identifier. The single-substance chemical identity remains UNCONFIRMED and the term stays in compound_identity_unverified, an administrative registration-state category, so this placement asserts no route/origin, natural occurrence, chemical class, synonym or regulatory status; the family scope recorded here is the term's documented scope, not a chemical-class link. Regulatory status is not claimed: no regulatory source was evaluated for this change, so the family confirmation neither sets nor implies one. Commercial products sold under this kind of label have shown severe label/content mismatches (Munger et al. 2026, DOI 10.26828/cannabis/2026/000348), and the 19-fold potency figure circulating in that market is an industry claim, not a measured human potency. **Query limitation:** Google Trends and YouTube series for `THCJD` / `THCJD リキッド` measure occurrences of those literal strings; `THCJD` is an acronym that can match unrelated text, and a matching result is not evidence of 3-octyl-THC content. Evidence that would move the term out of this category: a documented single chemical name with a verified external identifier, or a recorded source naming the single substance and its route/origin; the basis belongs in docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md.",
      "schema:termCode": "compound_thcjd"
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:DefinedTerm",
      "@id": "https://japan-cannabinoid-trends.routeflags.com/taxonomy/compound_thcpo",
      "schema:name": "THCPO",
      "schema:inDefinedTermSet": {
        "@type": "schema:DefinedTermSet",
        "@id": "https://japan-cannabinoid-trends.routeflags.com/taxonomy/compound-thcpo-scheme"
