      "schema:description": "`THCA-B` is the owner's search term, registered on 2026-11-11 (ISS-20261111-TAX-041) as one of three distinct search-topic concepts that replace the generic chemical interpretation of `THCA`; `compound_thca` remains the separate registration of the literal string `THCA`. The term is registered as a search string, not as a substance identification. The single-substance chemical identity remains UNCONFIRMED. The cited sources document the nomenclature and structure: Filer 2021 (DOI 10.1089/can.2021.0003, PMCID PMC8612408) records THCA-B as a structural isomer of THCA-A, first isolated and characterized by Mechoulam et al. in 1969 and corroborated by an X-ray study in 1975, and discusses its markedly different decarboxylation behaviour; Moreno-Sanz 2016 (DOI 10.1089/can.2016.0008) identifies THCA-B as 4-carboxy-THC, the minor isomer of THCA-A, and records that later studies could not confirm its occurrence. Those are statements about the published chemistry and about the literature, not a natural-occurrence claim by this repository, and not the content of any result. This concept carries no skos:exactMatch and no CAS number, PubChem CID, UNII, ChEBI ID or Wikidata QID: this change did not verify or select a single external record for the registered string, and the owner's confirmation fixes the query topic, not result-level substance identity. It stays in compound_identity_unverified, an administrative registration-state category, so the placement asserts no route/origin, natural occurrence, chemical class, structure, synonym or regulatory status. No skos:related link to compound_thca or another new concept is asserted; THCA-B is not a synonym of THCA-A, and the isomeric relation is recorded in this note only. **Query limitation:** Google Trends and YouTube series for `THCA-B` / `THCA-B リキッド` measure occurrences of those literal strings; a matching result is not evidence of THCA-B content. Evidence that would move the term out of this category: a verified external identifier for the named isomer (a documented chemical name with a verified identifier), after which the category that evidence supports can be assigned; the basis belongs in docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md.",
      "schema:termCode": "compound_thca_b"
    },
